    "Emergency exits open onto rumors of outside.": "2026-10-15",
    "Regarding parking meters, I have realized we are the only species that purchases time by the increment and then feels guilty about it.\n\nI put in two dollars for ninety minutes and then spent the entire lunch feeling the meter run, like a small mechanical pulse across the street, converting my money into permission and my permission into anxiety. I left at seventy minutes. I gifted the city twenty minutes of paid-for existence out of pure nerve failure.\n\nAnd here's the mechanism: the next car that pulls in inherits my twenty minutes and feels lucky. Someone always feels lucky. The whole system runs on the cowardice of people like me, and it's balanced beautifully, and I'll do it again Thursday.": "2026-10-16",
    "What does the vending machine do after closing?": "2026-10-17",
    "There are forty-one ketchup packets in my glovebox and I would like to walk you through the logic, because it isn't hoarding, it's insurance against a future I no longer believe in.\n\nEach one arrived with a meal I ate in a parking lot. Each one was surplus. Each one I declined to throw away because some part of me thought: there will be a day. A day with fries and no condiment. A day when a man with forty-one ketchup packets is the most prepared man in the county.\n\nThey're expiring. Ketchup expires. I checked one and it had turned a color I'd describe as \"brown decision.\" So now I'm carrying forty-one small sealed regrets and there's no bin appropriate for that.": "2026-10-18",
    "Grew up on a cul-de-sac and only recently learned it means \"bottom of the bag,\" which is French for the sack's end, which is a horrifying thing to name a place where children learn to ride bicycles.\n\nWe were raised at the bottom of a bag. Twelve houses arranged in a circle around nothing, a paved lung that inhaled cars and exhaled the same cars, no through traffic, no throughline. My parents called it safe. It was safe. It was also a closed loop, and every kid on that street either escaped by nineteen or is still there.\n\nI drove through last month. Turned around in the circle, as designed. That's the thing about a cul-de-sac: it doesn't stop you. It just makes leaving feel like your idea.": "2026-10-19",
    "Update on the hair ties: I bought sixty in November. Sixty. There are three in this apartment. I have conducted a search that I would describe as thorough and mildly unwell.\n\nThey do not break. That's the part nobody addresses. A hair tie does not wear out; it leaves. There's no graveyard, no drawer of casualties, no evidence. Fifty-seven elastic objects have exited a sealed dwelling and the doors were locked.\n\nMy working theory is that they're the same as socks and pens and the second earring, that there's a category of small object that has been quietly opting out for centuries and we've agreed to call it \"losing things\" because the alternative requires a longer conversation. I've bought sixty more. I'm not learning, I'm feeding something.": "2026-10-20",
    "I need to raise a concern about the flow chart. Every box leads somewhere. Every diamond offers yes or no. There is no box that says \"you will stand in the hallway for eleven minutes and then do nothing,\" which is the actual outcome of most processes at this company.\n\nWhoever draws these things believes decisions have two branches. I have never in my life encountered a decision with two branches. I have encountered a fog and a deadline.\n\nI've drafted an amended version with a single unlabeled arrow that loops back into itself. I think it's more accurate. I'm not going to present it, because there's no box for that.": "2026-10-21",
